WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.533 --> 00:00:18.866
I'm Alexander Rich. I'm going to tell you about experiments

00:00:18.867 --> 00:00:26.966
that occurred during the early years of molecular biology.

00:00:26.967 --> 00:00:34.332
I was a postdoctoral fellow with Linus Pauling from

00:00:34.333 --> 00:00:46.632
1949-1954, and during that period, at the end of it, he asked me

00:00:46.633 --> 00:00:57.632
to take x-ray diffraction photographs of DNA. The method for

00:00:57.633 --> 00:01:08.099
doing this is quite simple. DNA is usually a dry fibrous material,

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:16.332
you moisten it with water and put it on a glass slide. The

00:01:16.333 --> 00:01:24.599
glass slide is put on a microscope bed and attached to the

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:31.432
objective lens, you have a small thin glass rod. And using

00:01:31.433 --> 00:01:40.932
the drive of the microscope, you lower the glass rod

00:01:40.933 --> 00:01:48.432
till it touches this little gel-like material, and then you slowly

00:01:48.433 --> 00:01:53.132
withdraw it. And at the end, you're left with a glassy looking

00:01:53.133 --> 00:02:01.732
fiber. Since DNA is very elongated, you hope to have the molecules

00:02:01.733 --> 00:02:09.866
parallel to each other. And using x-rays, which are simply

00:02:09.867 --> 00:02:17.932
short wavelength light waves, but with wavelengths in the range

00:02:17.933 --> 00:02:25.266
of the distance between atoms. You get a diffraction pattern

00:02:25.267 --> 00:02:29.699
and from that you can infer something about the structure

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:37.666
of the molecule. Rosalind Franklin working in England carried out

00:02:37.667 --> 00:02:43.966
a very wonderful experiment in which she withdrew a fiber

00:02:43.967 --> 00:02:52.266
which was highly oriented and played an important role in allowing

00:02:52.267 --> 00:02:56.866
Watson and Crick to propose their double stranded model

00:02:56.867 --> 00:03:02.732
of DNA, in which the two strands are coiled around each other

00:03:02.733 --> 00:03:09.699
with the bases flat in the middle. The bases are what we call

00:03:09.700 --> 00:03:18.199
complementary, that is adenine pairs with thymine, and guanine

00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:23.699
pairs with cytosine. These bases stack on each other in the

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:34.632
center. When I started working, the Watson and Crick paper was

00:03:34.633 --> 00:03:39.099
published. And I could immediately see that this was correct.

00:03:39.100 --> 00:03:51.666
And I then asked myself the following question at a meeting at Caltech,

00:03:51.667 --> 00:04:00.866
where all the DNA people were there. I asked myself, what about

00:04:00.867 --> 00:04:08.499
RNA? In RNA, you have the same four bases, except the

00:04:08.500 --> 00:04:15.566
thymine which pairs with adenine, is usually replaced by uracil.

00:04:15.567 --> 00:04:26.466
But again, complementary. So after this meeting, I started

00:04:26.467 --> 00:04:33.899
working with RNA samples. Withdrawing fibers to see if I could get

00:04:33.900 --> 00:04:40.932
an oriented pattern. Jim Watson, who was at Caltech at the same time,

00:04:40.933 --> 00:04:45.299
was interested in the problem, so we worked together on this.

00:04:45.300 --> 00:04:56.166
And during a good part of 1954, we photographed a large number

00:04:56.167 --> 00:05:00.866
of RNA preparations that we obtained from different people.

00:05:00.867 --> 00:05:07.466
All of them were frustrating, they did not show evidence

00:05:07.467 --> 00:05:13.832
of orientation. And so we could not say whether it could form a

00:05:13.833 --> 00:05:26.899
double helix or not. At the end of 1954, I went to NIH to

00:05:26.900 --> 00:05:32.532
set up my own lab and continued working on this problem.

00:05:32.533 --> 00:05:40.799
Around that time, methods were made, enzymes were isolated

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:50.299
namely by Severo Ochoa and Marianne Grunberg-Manago, which

00:05:50.300 --> 00:05:56.866
isolated an enzyme which would take ribonucleotides and polymerize

00:05:56.867 --> 00:06:03.799
them into chains. So you could make polyriboadenylic acid

00:06:03.800 --> 00:06:16.266
and polyribouridylic acid. I studied these compounds

00:06:16.267 --> 00:06:21.499
together with David Davies, who would join me at NIH.

00:06:21.500 --> 00:06:29.699
And we found they had very little organization within them.

00:06:29.700 --> 00:06:42.666
But in early 1956, for reasons that were not very clear to me,

00:06:42.667 --> 00:06:51.466
I decided to mix together polyriboadenylic acid and polyribouridylic

00:06:51.467 --> 00:07:00.366
acid. Immediately I noted that the gel became rather cloudy

00:07:00.367 --> 00:07:08.166
and much more viscous. And so I put the glass fiber in and

00:07:08.167 --> 00:07:20.632
carefully withdrew a fiber of the nucleic acid. And put it in

00:07:20.633 --> 00:07:25.932
the x-ray beam, and to my astonishment, there was the diffraction

00:07:25.933 --> 00:07:31.466
pattern of the double helix. Not the DNA double helix, a slightly different

00:07:31.467 --> 00:07:42.266
form. And we then carried out several analyses of this, and what we

00:07:42.267 --> 00:07:50.866
concluded was that the molecules had found each other

00:07:50.867 --> 00:07:57.199
and combined to hybridize. The word "hybridization" was

00:07:57.200 --> 00:08:04.066
not used at that time. It was used later, but it was meant to

00:08:04.067 --> 00:08:12.766
describe the binding together of complementary oligonucleotides.

00:08:12.767 --> 00:08:25.566
Complementary either DNA or RNA. So, all of a sudden,

00:08:25.567 --> 00:08:35.799
we wrote up a short article or letter to the Journal of the American

00:08:35.800 --> 00:08:42.999
Chemical Society describing this remarkable event. That there were two

00:08:43.000 --> 00:08:49.032
issues here, 1. Could RNA form a double helix? The answer

00:08:49.033 --> 00:08:56.832
was definitively yes. 2. This remarkable phenomenon

00:08:56.833 --> 00:09:05.499
of the hybridization, or spontaneous formation, of a double helix

00:09:05.500 --> 00:09:15.999
from individual complementary chains. Many people were very skeptical

00:09:16.000 --> 00:09:27.332
of that interpretation. Partly they said, look, these chains are very long,

00:09:27.333 --> 00:09:33.799
they're likely to get entangled. How would they untangle and combine?

00:09:33.800 --> 00:09:41.832
And these chains are all negatively charged, they have phosphate

00:09:41.833 --> 00:09:46.699
groups in them. How could those negative charges come together?

00:09:46.700 --> 00:10:02.232
And finally, 3. An argument concerning thermodynamics. In general,

00:10:02.233 --> 00:10:07.832
things do not go from disordered to ordered. They go from

00:10:07.833 --> 00:10:15.332
order to disorder. How could this occur? Well, what was not obvious

00:10:15.333 --> 00:10:21.366
to everybody, but became obvious to me after studying it,

00:10:21.367 --> 00:10:27.166
is that the people who made these arguments did not understand

00:10:27.167 --> 00:10:35.999
the entropic, the randomization, associated with shedding

00:10:36.000 --> 00:10:39.399
water molecules from these chains when they combined.

00:10:39.400 --> 00:10:50.366
Now what drives the phenomenon? What drives the phenomenon

00:10:50.367 --> 00:11:01.166
is the fact that these bases, adenine and uracil, or guanine and

00:11:01.167 --> 00:11:09.199
cytosine, they are essentially lipid soluble, they like to live in oils

00:11:09.200 --> 00:11:15.666
not in water, because of their flat ring surface. So what happens

00:11:15.667 --> 00:11:22.132
is you bring these together, forming a molecule, a double stranded

00:11:22.133 --> 00:11:27.499
molecule which is essentially oily in the middle. And that's a very strong

00:11:27.500 --> 00:11:37.899
force for bringing them together. Around that time in 1956,

00:11:37.900 --> 00:11:50.732
there was a meeting in Baltimore of the McCollum Pratt dealing with genetics.

00:11:50.733 --> 00:11:56.699
And I presented this material. And at the end of my talk, a

00:11:56.700 --> 00:12:03.432
middle-aged Englishman in a tweed suit came up to me

00:12:03.433 --> 00:12:08.332
and said, "Professor Rich, I want to congratulate you. You've

00:12:08.333 --> 00:12:13.532
discovered molecular sex." This was Julian Huxley, a

00:12:13.533 --> 00:12:20.432
science writer with a very vivid imagination. And it was his way of

00:12:20.433 --> 00:12:29.366
describing the specificity of hybridization. The hybridization

00:12:29.367 --> 00:12:37.532
has become a major tool in the development of molecular

00:12:37.533 --> 00:12:43.866
biology. For example, a well-known reaction called the polymerase

00:12:43.867 --> 00:12:51.899
chain reaction, in which you put together a system and essentially

00:12:51.900 --> 00:12:58.066
increase the amount of DNA starting from very little. This makes

00:12:58.067 --> 00:13:08.532
possible all kinds of medical and biological experiments. It is

00:13:08.533 --> 00:13:16.132
based on the combination of a single strand of DNA with a

00:13:16.133 --> 00:13:23.199
complementary small primer of 20 nucleotides or so. That

00:13:23.200 --> 00:13:28.632
combination occurs spontaneously because of hybridization.

00:13:28.633 --> 00:13:38.832
And it allows the whole process to continue. It's very difficult

00:13:38.833 --> 00:13:45.099
to underestimate the extent to which hybridization dominates

00:13:45.100 --> 00:13:54.299
a great deal of the developments that have been at the basis

00:13:54.300 --> 00:14:02.099
of molecular biology. They're used extensively and are used broadly.

00:14:02.100 --> 00:14:32.200
And are still so today. Thank you.

